# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROFILE IS glm47flash AND NOT glm47, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A DIFFERENT
# MODEL. zai-org publishes BOTH `GLM-4.7` and `GLM-4.7-Flash`, and they are
# not two names for one thing: the Flash is the 30B-A3B MoE this profile
# serves, the other is the large sibling. Every file, unit and variant name
# here carries `flash` for that reason, and so does every sentence below —
# "GLM-4.7" alone in this file would always be a mistake, never shorthand.
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=GLM-4.7-Flash-30B-A3B · Q4_K_M, 17.6 GiB · Vulkan, n-gram only, 28.9 GiB GTT · THE RECOMMENDED second-family sparring partner beside qwen36 (04.09.2026, 19:45) — gemma26 was measured after this and is faster, cheaper and better on published scores, but loses the middle of a long context (40/48 against this model's 48/48); JUDGEMENT is still the thing this repo cannot measure, and on published numbers this model is last of the three
# MODEL_SWA=no — MEASURED, not assumed: config.json of zai-org/GLM-4.7-Flash
# (fetched 04.09.2026) has no `sliding_window` key, and the chat template
# shipped inside the GGUF (read the same day out of the Hugging Face API's
# `gguf` metadata, 3,120 chars) contains no window either. Every one of the
# 47 layers keeps a real KV — what makes the cache small here is MLA, not a
# window (see MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN).
MODEL_SWA=no
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched.
#
# NOT ggml-org AND NOT unsloth, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS LINE.
# The checkpoint has an MTP head (`num_nextn_predict_layers: 1`), and BOTH of
# the obvious publishers drop it. Read 04.09.2026 out of the GGUF headers
# themselves, over a range request, before any download: ggml-org's Q4_K and
# unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL each carry 844 tensors, `deepseek2.block_count 47`,
# blk indices 0..46, no `nextn_predict_layers` key and no `blk.*.nextn.*`
# tensor. meshllm's file carries 868 tensors, `block_count 48`, blk 0..47,
# `nextn_predict_layers 1` and all six `blk.47.nextn.*` tensors — plus
# `token_embd.weight`, which is the check `defects.json` ->
# `qwen36-sidecar-mtp-head-has-no-embeddings` exists for.
#
# THE PRICE IS PROVENANCE, and it is the same price qwen36 pays: no published
# imatrix, so the file is trusted for its STRUCTURE, which was read off it,
# and not for its calibration, which nobody can check.
MODEL_SOURCE=meshllm/GLM-4.7-Flash-MTP-GGUF MTP-Q4_K_M
# THE Q8 COMPARISON FILE comes from a DIFFERENT publisher, because no single
# one ships this model with the head at two precisions:
#   jacek2024/GLM-4.7-Flash-MTP-GGUF  GLM-4.7-Flash-MTP-Q8_0.gguf
# 868 tensors, block_count 48, nextn_predict_layers 1, token_embd.weight
# present — verified the same way and the same day. That the two quants come
# from two quantisers is a CONFOUND and it is named where the comparison is
# reported: it does not disturb a throughput reading, which is about how many
# weight bytes cross the bus, but it means the pair says nothing about which
# file is the better-calibrated one.
# TEMPLATE_LEVELS — which reasoning levels this model's chat template renders.
# `no-levels` is MEASURED, not defaulted: the template inside the GGUF (read
# 04.09.2026 through the Hugging Face API's `gguf` metadata) contains
# `enable_thinking` twice and `clear_thinking` twice, and neither
# `reasoning_effort` nor `thinking_budget` at all. Thinking is a switch here,
# not a dial, so a mode naming a level would be silently dropped by the
# template; setup/gateway/modes.py refuses one instead.
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=no-levels
# Every vocabulary word maps to the one thing the template understands — the
# same shape qwen36, gemma26 and batch use, and for the same reason.
MODES=low:on  medium:on  high:on  xhigh:on  max:on
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — MEASURED 04.09.2026 by the two-point method: load
# at -c 65536 and at -c 202752 and take the GTT difference, so the weights and
# the compute buffers cancel out of the subtraction rather than having to be
# estimated. Both points behind bench/sideserver.py on
# build-rocm-cachelookup-b10750-10-g6bbb3eecf, on the MTP-Q8_0 file, in the
# shape this profile serves:
#
#     -c  65536   GTT (own, per DRM client) 37.77 GiB   RssAnon 0.26
#     -c 202752   GTT                       52.16 GiB   RssAnon 0.28
#
#     14.39 GiB over 137,216 tokens = 109.97 KiB per token
#     -> 21.26 GiB of KV at the full window
#
# THE BASE COMPUTED FROM EACH POINT INDEPENDENTLY AGREES TO TWO DECIMALS:
# 37.77 - 6.87 = 30.90 and 52.16 - 21.26 = 30.90. That agreement is the
# check, not a coincidence — it is what says the model is linear in the window
# and that neither point was taken in a disturbed state.
#
# THE FIGURE DEPENDS ON WHETHER AN MTP DRAFT CONTEXT IS RUNNING, and that is
# the finding rather than a footnote. FOUR points, two independent pairs, each
# pair agreeing with itself on the base:
#
#                       -c 65536   -c 202752   KiB/token   base    KV @full
#     no speculation      33.00       40.20        55.0     29.56   10.64
#     draft-mtp,ngram     37.77       52.16       110.0     30.90   21.26
#
# **EXACTLY DOUBLE.** llama.cpp's MTP draft context allocates a SECOND,
# FULL-SIZE KV cache — the server announces it as `creating MTP draft context
# against the target model` and the arithmetic then says what that costs. The
# head is 11.96 GiB of KV at the full window plus 1.34 GiB of base, and
# **it buys nothing on prose** (see SPECULATION). This profile therefore does
# not run it, and the declared figure above is the no-MTP one — the shape this
# profile serves.
#
# THE DERIVATION FROM THE CONFIG WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE MODEL AND WRONG ABOUT
# llama.cpp. The config declares MLA (kv_lora_rank 512 + qk_rope 64 =
# key_length 576) and llama.cpp's `has_v = !is_mla` (llama-kv-cache.cpp:231)
# allocates NO V cache under MLA, giving 47 x 1 x 576 x 2 B = 52.9 KiB/token.
# Against the measured no-MTP figure of 55.0 that is **4 % low**, the closest
# a derivation has come on this machine. It read as 52 % low for one hour,
# against the MTP measurement, and the error was entirely in assuming one KV
# cache where the draft path keeps two.
#
# FOR SCALE, all measured here: qwen36 21.0 KiB/token, flashnext 37.3,
# qwen38 74.3. Even at 55.0 this model's cache is the second most expensive
# in the repo and 2.6x qwen36's — 10.64 GiB at its full window against
# qwen36's 5.25 GiB at a window 29 % LONGER. That difference is visible in
# the decode numbers and grows with depth.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=55.0
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=04.09.2026, TWO two-point pairs behind bench/sideserver.py on the MTP-Q8_0 file. Without speculation (the shape this profile serves): GTT 33.00 GiB at -c 65536 and 40.20 at -c 202752, i.e. 7.20 GiB over 137,216 tokens = 55.02 KiB per token, base 29.56 from both points independently. With --spec-type draft-mtp,ngram-mod: 37.77 and 52.16, i.e. 109.97 KiB per token, base 30.90 from both points - EXACTLY DOUBLE, because the MTP draft context allocates a second full-size KV cache. Raw output in bench/reports/2026-09-04_1440_glm47flash-memory/memory-observations.txt. The derivation from the checkpoint's config beforehand was 52.9, which is 4 % under the no-MTP measurement - the closest a derivation has come on this machine.
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — what GTT shows with the KV taken out, i.e. the weights
# the GPU really pins plus its compute buffers. Computed from EACH of the two
# points above independently; they agree at 30.90.
#
# NOTE IT IS 0.02 GiB BELOW the 30.92 GiB file size, i.e. essentially the
# whole file and nothing measurable on top. That is a different picture from
# qwen36, whose MTP-Q4 came out 0.80 GiB ABOVE its file because the draft
# graph needs its own buffers. Here the MTP draft context was demonstrably
# created — the server says `creating MTP draft context against the target
# model` in both loads — and costs nothing visible in GTT at this resolution.
# Not explained; recorded as observed.
MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB=29.56
# MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB — what stays resident OUTSIDE GTT. RssAnon of the
# RUNNING server, the one-line check this repo requires: 0.26 GiB at -c 65536
# and 0.28 at -c 202752, over four loads on 04.09.2026. The declared value is
# the largest. Re-measure if LLAMA_BIN moves: 28.6 GiB was found where 0.31
# was declared on 31.08., which is how that rule got written.
MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB=0.22
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS MODEL IS HERE
# ----------------------
# The operator asked for maximum throughput at sufficient quality. flashnext
# is the incumbent — 125B total, 6B active, 103.7 GiB of file, 91.0 GiB
# measured in GTT. qwen36 was integrated and measured on 04.09.2026 as the
# first candidate: 1.9-2.9x on all twelve cells at 27.7 GiB. This is the
# second candidate on the same axis — 30B total, 3B active, MLA attention,
# and its own MTP head.
#
# WHAT IT IS, from the checkpoint's own config.json (fetched 04.09.2026 from
# zai-org/GLM-4.7-Flash; every figure below is a READ, not a measurement):
#
#     architectures              Glm4MoeLiteForCausalLM
#     model_type                 glm4_moe_lite
#     num_hidden_layers          47
#     hidden_size                2048
#     num_attention_heads        20,  num_key_value_heads 20
#     q_lora_rank                768, kv_lora_rank 512
#     qk_nope_head_dim           192, qk_rope_head_dim 64, v_head_dim 256
#     n_routed_experts           64,  num_experts_per_tok 4
#     n_shared_experts           1,   moe_intermediate_size 1536
#     first_k_dense_replace      1
#     max_position_embeddings    202752
#     num_nextn_predict_layers   1
#     vocab_size                 154880
#
# THE ARCHITECTURE TAG SAYS deepseek2, AND THAT IS NOT A MISTAKE — it is the
# whole reason this integration cost no build. GLM-4.7-Flash carries MLA and
# DeepSeek-style MoE routing, so llama.cpp's converter maps it straight onto
# the existing graph: `conversion/glm.py:249` registers
# `Glm4MoeLiteForCausalLM` with `model_arch = gguf.MODEL_ARCH.DEEPSEEK2`.
# Consequence: **no new llama.cpp build is needed.**
#
# CHECKED AGAINST THE BUILT ARTIFACT rather than the source, because a tree
# and a binary are different things, and checked against the GGUF's own
# declaration rather than against the converter's intent:
#
#   * the file says so — `general.architecture = deepseek2`, read 04.09.2026
#     out of the published GGUF header (both ggml-org's and meshllm's)
#   * the binaries know it — `strings bin/libllama.so | grep -c '^deepseek2$'`
#     is 1 in build-rocm-cachelookup-b10750-10-g6bbb3eecf (the pin below), in
#     build-rocm-patched-b10702-11-gc799f1014 (the house build), and in
#     build-vulkan
#
# AND THE MTP PATH IS IN THAT GRAPH TOO, which was the second thing that
# could have cost a build. `src/models/deepseek2.cpp` builds a full nextn
# graph with MLA (`mtp_kv_cmpr`, `mtp_q_nope_absorbed`), so a file carrying
# the head is servable with `--spec-type draft-mtp` and needs no sidecar.
# NOTE the assertion string in that file reads "GLM4 MTP" — a mislabelled
# message in the deepseek2 path, not evidence that it is somebody else's code.
#
# --- the arguments, and what is and is not known about each -----------------
#
# MEASURED 04.09.2026, six cells, bench/reports/2026-09-04_1509_sweep_glm47flash.
# The launch line below is NOT the one this file started with: the sweep
# removed the MTP head and moved the backend.
#
# -np 1: NOT a fresh decision, and the one flag here that arrived justified.
#   Two independent defects were measured on this hardware at two slots
#   (setup/env/qwen38.env carries the matrix): the gfx1151 HIP race
#   (llama.cpp #27579) and slot-restore poisoning. Neither is model-specific,
#   so a new model does not get to reopen the question by default.
# -c 202752: the model's own max_position_embeddings, and CHECKED against the
#   measured KV rather than the estimate — 55.0 KiB/token x 202,752 = 10.64
#   GiB, which is what the observed GTT shows. Affordable, but note it is
#   2.6x what qwen36 pays per token.
#
# BACKEND: VULKAN, AND IT IS NOT CLOSE. Six cells at -c 65536, decode t/s on
# the workload no drafter can help — `prose`, the floor of everything this
# machine does, and the one column where every cell came back without
# speed.py's spread warning:
#
#                      d512   d8192   d36k
#     rocm-nospec      29.6    25.3    17.3
#     vulkan-nospec    45.7    34.3    19.2      <- this profile
#     rocm-ngram       29.9    23.5    17.0
#     vulkan-ngram     45.1    29.8    18.7
#     rocm-mtp         29.7    24.3    15.9
#     vulkan-mtp       39.7    25.0    11.2
#
#   Vulkan wins the floor by 1.54x / 1.36x / 1.11x. That is the same
#   direction qwen36 measured (1.45x) and the same direction an operator
#   handover reports from other Strix Halo machines. Unlike qwen36, ROCm does
#   NOT take it back anywhere reliable here, so the backend question is not
#   split for this model.
#
#   AND THE VULKAN ARM IS STILL HANDICAPPED TWICE, both understating it:
#   build-vulkan is commit 54ee5ee of 22.08. against the ROCm arm's
#   b10750-10, on a stack where a build difference has been worth 25 %; and
#   the RADV APU heap option is not set on this machine at all (no drirc
#   anywhere, Mesa 26.1.7). So this is a floor on Vulkan's margin.
#
# SPECULATION: THE N-GRAM DRAFTER ONLY. THE MODEL'S OWN MTP HEAD IS REJECTED,
# and that is the surprise of the day.
#
#   The file carries the head (see MODEL_SOURCE), llama.cpp builds its graph
#   (`creating MTP draft context against the target model` in every load),
#   and it DRAFTS WELL — 82.2 / 59.4 / 78.1 % acceptance on prose under ROCm,
#   88.2 / 29.1 / 33.7 % under Vulkan. On qwen36 the same mechanism at LOWER
#   acceptance (45-49 %) was worth 15-29 % on prose and was the largest single
#   lever measured all day.
#
#   HERE IT IS WORTH NOTHING, AND ON VULKAN IT IS WORTH LESS THAN NOTHING:
#
#     prose      rocm            vulkan
#     d512    29.6 -> 29.7    45.7 -> 39.7   (-13 %)
#     d8192   25.3 -> 24.3    34.3 -> 25.0   (-27 %)
#     d36k    17.3 -> 15.9    19.2 -> 11.2   (-42 %)
#
#   plus 11.96 GiB of KV at the full window and 1.34 of base, because the
#   draft context keeps a second full-size cache (see MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN).
#   A drafter that accepts three quarters of what it writes and still loses
#   throughput is one whose VERIFICATION costs what it saves; this model's KV
#   is 2.6x qwen36's per token, which is the obvious candidate and is NOT
#   established here — nothing in this sweep isolates it.
#
#   THE N-GRAM DRAFTER STAYS, and it earns its place on the two columns the
#   head could not: vulkan-ngram against vulkan-nospec, on the cells that came
#   back WITHOUT a spread warning — count at d8192, 126.7 against 34.1, and
#   copy at d36k, 25.0 against 19.4. It costs 1-13 % of the prose floor
#   (45.1/29.8/18.7 against 45.7/34.3/19.2), which is the same trade qwen36
#   makes and the same reason. It costs NOTHING in GTT: the sweep read 34.1
#   GiB for both the nospec and the ngram cell at -c 65536.
#
#   READ THE SPECULATED CELLS WITH CARE, AND MOSTLY DO NOT. 19 of this
#   sweep's 54 cells carry speed.py's spread warning, and all 19 are
#   speculated count or copy cells — rocm-ngram's count ranged 30.2-264.6 t/s
#   over three runs at d512. On this model the speculated ceiling is not a
#   quantity this instrument can resolve; the floor is.
#
# -ub 512 -b 2048: carried, and carried for a reason that is about this
#   MACHINE rather than about this model — prompt-cache reuse is quantized to
#   the ubatch, measured twice here (qwen38 98.7 % against 94.5 %; qwen36
#   94.7 % against 55.5 % at d8192, and 2.2-7.2x on what a warm turn actually
#   costs). NOT re-confirmed in this model's serving shape.
# -fa on, -fit off, -cram 32768: carried from the house shape, unmeasured
#   here. -fit off is not a tuning but a refusal to let the fitter guess
#   (flashnext.env carries the crash report it comes from).
# --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false}: thinking OFF by default,
#   with MODES above able to turn it on per request. NOTE this model's
#   published figures are THINKING figures (AIME 25 at 91.6), so the quality
#   question and this flag are entangled in a way they were not for qwen36.
# -m: THE Q4_K_M, AND THE QUANT COMPARISON IS THE REASON. Measured 04.09.2026
#   in this serving shape, one variable, both arms behind sideserver.py —
#   reports 2026-09-04_1612_speed_glm47flash-q8-vulkan-ngram and
#   …_1628_speed_glm47flash-q4-vulkan-ngram-tpl. Decode t/s on `prose`, the
#   only column where NEITHER arm carries a spread warning:
#
#                  Q8_0    Q4_K_M   Q4/Q8
#     prose d512   45.6     63.3     1.39
#     prose d8192  33.8     36.1     1.07
#     prose d36k   14.0     21.7     1.55
#
#   plus GTT 28.9 GiB against 41.7. Prefill is a wash (1.05 / 1.01 / 1.00x).
#   count and copy carry a warning in at least one arm at every depth and are
#   NOT comparable here.
#
#   NOTE THIS DOES NOT REPEAT qwen36's FINDING. There Q8 cost 5-8 % and the
#   fidelity was nearly free; here Q4 is worth 7-55 % and the trade is real.
#   Same machine, same day, same instrument — so "doubling the weights is
#   nearly free on this box" is not a property of the box.
#
# --chat-template-file: NOT A TUNING. THE Q4 FILE HAS NO CHAT TEMPLATE.
#   `tokenizer.chat_template` is absent from meshllm's GGUF — not empty,
#   absent — while the same model's Q8_0 carries 3,120 characters of it.
#   llama-server does not fail on that: it substitutes a built-in default and
#   serves. What that cost, measured before the cause was found:
#
#                                    fallback      with the template
#     copy, share actually copied   3.1/7.9/0.0 %   88.9/96.2/88.9 %
#     prose, n-gram acceptance      72.0 % @d512    0.0 %
#     prompt length, same prompts   +16 tokens      exactly the Q8's
#
#   The n-gram figure is the one to remember. That drafter drafts FROM THE
#   PROMPT, so high acceptance on NOVEL prose means the model is repeating it
#   — a degeneracy meter that happened to be running. The first reading of
#   this run had Q4 at 1.60x on prose and it was the speed of worse output.
#   setup/defects.json carries it as `glm47flash-q4-gguf-has-no-chat-template`.
#
#   The template is extracted from the Q8_0 of the SAME model and lives in
#   setup/templates/; install.sh links the directory into ~/.local/lib/llm-
#   stack so the unit reaches it without knowing where the checkout sits.
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias glm47flash -m @MODELS@/GLM-4.7-Flash-MTP-Q4_K_M.gguf \
  --chat-template-file @HOME@/.local/lib/llm-stack/templates/glm47flash.jinja \
  --spec-type ngram-mod --spec-draft-n-max 4 \
  --spec-ngram-mod-n-min 24 --spec-draft-p-min 0.75 \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -fit off -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 202752 -np 1 -cram 32768 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --slot-save-path @HOME@/.cache/llama-slots \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
# NOT SET, AND THE REASON IS THAT IT WOULD BE THEATRE. It exists in other
# profiles because the MTP path on ROCm below 7.13 needs HIP graphs off; this
# profile serves Vulkan and no MTP head, so the variable addresses neither
# half of its own justification. Note also, measured 31.08.2026 and recorded
# in bench/sideserver.py's own docstring: a profile's env vars never reach a
# side server anyway, so EVERY figure in this file was taken without it.
# The pinned build, carried from qwen36.env with its reasoning intact: it is
# post-#27941, which is what setup/defects.json ->
# `flashnext-mtp-serving-shape-hangs` puts in the condition for a draft-mtp
# serving shape, and it is what flashnext has served MTP from since 02.09.
# UNTESTED ON THIS MODEL. The retirement condition is qwen36's, unchanged:
# the pin goes away when the house build is post-#27941, loads this file, and
# one depth-correctness run on this model's serving shape comes back green.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-vulkan/bin/llama-server
#
# --- open points, in the order they block something -------------------------
#
#   [x] DOES IT LOAD AT ALL — yes, and it cost nothing. `deepseek2`, declared
#       by the file and present in all three artifacts, MTP graph included.
#       Answered 04.09.2026 before a single byte was downloaded.
#   [x] IS THERE AN MTP HEAD TO SERVE — yes, and not from the two obvious
#       publishers. See MODEL_SOURCE.
#   [x] MEMORY — two two-point pairs, each agreeing with itself on the base.
#       55.0 KiB/token without speculation, 110.0 with the MTP head, exactly
#       double. See MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN.
#   [x] SPECULATION — measured six ways. The n-gram drafter stays; the
#       model's own MTP head is REJECTED, and it is the surprise of the day:
#       it accepts 59-82 % of its drafts and costs throughput.
#   [x] BACKEND — Vulkan, and unlike qwen36 not split.
#   [x] QUANT — Q4_K_M, 1.07-1.55x the Q8_0 on the warning-free column, at
#       28.9 GiB against 41.7. NOT the 5-8 % qwen36 measured.
#   [x] SPEED AGAINST flashnext, live and the same day (17:12, against
#       production on port 8080): 1.45-3.10x on prose at 28.9 GiB of GTT
#       against 91.6 — and 0.36x on `copy` at d36k, the only warning-free
#       speculated cell, which is the shape most agent output takes.
#   [x] depth-correctness — GREEN, 24 of 24 to 99,113 tokens on the exact
#       serving shape. bench/reports/2026-09-04_1646_depth-correctness_
#       glm47flash-q4-vulkan-ngram. It rules out a BROKEN quant, not a WORSE
#       one; meshllm publishes no imatrix and that point stands.
#
#   [~] THE VERDICT WAS WRITTEN AGAINST THE WRONG STANDARD AND IS REVISED.
#       Until 17:50 on 04.09. this file said "not recommended", because it
#       compared the model against qwen36 for qwen36's seat. That is not the
#       arrangement: **qwen36 is the production model** (switched 17:44) and
#       this one is wanted BESIDE it as a sparring partner from a different
#       family. Against THAT standard "slower than qwen36" is expected rather
#       than disqualifying — a critic does not carry the interactive load —
#       and the measurements answer the role's questions in the affirmative:
#       genuinely different family, prose 63.3/36.1/21.7 t/s, 28.9 GiB beside
#       qwen36's 27.8 under a 108 GiB cap, depth-correctness 24 of 24.
#       WHAT REMAINS AGAINST IT IS THE ONE THING THIS REPO CANNOT MEASURE:
#       judgement. The published figures put it below qwen36 AND below Gemma 4
#       on every shared axis (GPQA-D 75.2 against 86.0 and 82.3; LiveCodeBench
#       v6 64.0 against 80.4 and 77.1 — vendor numbers, read 04.09.2026, NOT
#       measured here). For a CRITIC that gap is the whole point rather than a
#       detail: one that judges worse than the model it reviews produces noise
#       instead of disagreement.
#       SO: viable, and not the first to try. `gemma26` is already installed,
#       is QAT-q4_0 rather than an imatrix-less third-party quant, beats this
#       model on every published axis they share, and has an unpulled lever in
#       Google's own 0.30 GiB draft model. Measure it before choosing.
#       Full account: bench/reports/2026-09-04_1730_glm47flash-vs-flashnext,
#       sections 8 and 9.
#
#   [x] gemma26 WAS MEASURED, 18:11-19:36 ON 04.09.2026, AND THIS PROFILE WINS
#       — which is not what anyone expected, this file included. Every hope for
#       gemma26 was borne out: Google's own drafter works and is nearly free
#       (1.60/1.29/1.11x on prose, +2.03 KiB/token, no second KV cache unlike
#       the head above), Vulkan wins all six sweep cells, and it is 1.5-2.1x
#       PRODUCTION qwen36 on prose at 17.2 GiB of GTT against this model's
#       28.9. It lost on one thing and it is the one that decides a critic:
#       **depth-correctness 40/48 against this model's 48/48**, first wrong at
#       51,728 tokens, and the failure survives removing the drafter, adding
#       --swa-full, and switching to ROCm (where it is worse, 36/48 from
#       35,843). A sparring partner that silently loses the middle of what you
#       hand it produces noise rather than disagreement — which is the same
#       sentence that was written against THIS model on published scores, now
#       measured against the other one.
#       WHAT STILL STANDS AGAINST THIS PROFILE IS UNCHANGED: judgement, which
#       this repo cannot measure, and where the published numbers put it last
#       of the three. That has not improved; it has been outweighed.
#       Full account: bench/reports/2026-09-04_1905_gemma26-vs-qwen36/REPORT.md,
#       defect: setup/defects.json -> gemma26-loses-the-middle-of-a-long-context.
#
#   [ ] STILL OPEN, and none of it blocks the verdict above:
#       * WHY the MTP head loses. "Verification costs what it saves at 2.6x
#         the KV per token" is the obvious candidate and nothing here
#         isolates it. A --spec-draft-n-max sweep would speak to it.
#       * -ub 512 / -b 2048, -cram 32768, -fa on, -fit off: all carried, none
#         re-confirmed in this model's serving shape.
#       * The Vulkan handicap: an eight-day-older build and no RADV APU heap
#         option on this machine. Both understate Vulkan, and Vulkan already
#         wins here, so closing them can only widen the margin — it cannot
#         reach qwen36's 37.3 at d36k from 21.7.
#       * PROVENANCE: meshllm publishes no imatrix, and this file needed a
#         chat template extracted from a different publisher's GGUF to behave
#         at all. `conversion/glm.py:249` carries supports_mtp_export, so the
#         repo could convert the checkpoint itself — 48 shards, ~57 GiB, plus
#         a build shipping llama-quantize. Not tried, and not worth trying
#         unless the verdict above is revisited.
